{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $k=3$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2k-6$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{6}{2k - 6} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n(2k-6)\\cdot \\dfrac{6}{2k - 6} \u0026=(2k-6)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n6\u0026=\\dfrac23 k-2\n\\\\\\\\\n8\u0026=\\dfrac23 k\n\\\\\\\\\n12\u0026=k\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $k=12$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $k=12$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $k$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{6}{2k - 6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n$k =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":12}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
